#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:3
msgid "Building Extension Modules"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:5
msgid ""
"Setuptools can build C/C++ extension modules.  The keyword argument "
"``ext_modules`` of ``setup()`` should be a list of instances of the :class:"
"`setuptools.Extension` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:10
msgid ""
"For example, let's consider a simple project with only one extension module::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:12
msgid ""
"<project_folder>\n"
"├── pyproject.toml\n"
"└── foo.c"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:16
msgid "and all project metadata configuration in the ``pyproject.toml`` file:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:18
msgid ""
"# pyproject.toml\n"
"[build-system]\n"
"requires = [\"setuptools\"]\n"
"build-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n"
"\n"
"[project]\n"
"name = \"mylib-foo\"  # as it would appear on PyPI\n"
"version = \"0.42\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:29
msgid ""
"To instruct setuptools to compile the ``foo.c`` file into the extension "
"module ``mylib.foo``, we need to define an appropriate configuration in "
"either ``pyproject.toml`` [#pyproject.toml]_ or ``setup.py`` file , similar "
"to the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:36
msgid ""
"[tool.setuptools]\n"
"ext-modules = [\n"
"  {name = \"mylib.foo\", sources = [\"foo.c\"]}\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:45
msgid ""
"from setuptools import Extension, setup\n"
"\n"
"setup(\n"
"    ext_modules=[\n"
"        Extension(\n"
"            name=\"mylib.foo\",\n"
"            sources=[\"foo.c\"],\n"
"        ),\n"
"    ]\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:58
msgid ""
"The ``name`` value corresponds to how the extension module would be imported "
"and may include packages/namespaces separated by ``.``. The ``sources`` "
"value is a list of all source files that are compiled into a single binary "
"file. Optionally any other parameter of :class:`setuptools.Extension` can be "
"defined in the configuration file (but in the case of ``pyproject.toml`` "
"they must be written using :wiki:`kebab-case` convention)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:67
msgid ""
"``Extension(..., py_limited_api=True)`` and wheel tagging are configured "
"separately. If the built wheel should be tagged with ``abi3`` for CPython's "
"limited API, configure the ``bdist_wheel`` ``py_limited_api`` option with "
"the minimum supported CPython tag, such as ``cp311``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:74
msgid ""
"[bdist_wheel]\n"
"py-limited-api = cp311"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:81
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    options={\"bdist_wheel\": {\"py_limited_api\": \"cp311\"}},\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:88
msgid ""
"You can find more information on the `Python docs about C/C++ extensions`_. "
"Alternatively, you might also be interested in learning about `Cython`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:91
msgid ""
"If you plan to distribute a package that uses extensions across multiple "
"platforms, :pypi:`cibuildwheel` can also be helpful."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:95
msgid ""
"All files used to compile your extension need to be available on the system "
"when building the package, so please make sure to include some documentation "
"on how developers interested in building your package from source can obtain "
"operating system level dependencies (e.g. compilers and external binary "
"libraries/artifacts)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:101
msgid ""
"You will also need to make sure that all auxiliary files that are contained "
"inside your :term:`project` (e.g. C headers authored by you or your team) "
"are configured to be included in your :term:`sdist <Source Distribution (or "
"\"sdist\")>`. Please have a look on our section on :ref:`Controlling files "
"in the distribution`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:108
msgid "Compiler and linker options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:110
msgid ""
"The command ``build_ext`` builds C/C++ extension modules.  It creates a "
"command line for running the compiler and linker by combining compiler and "
"linker options from various sources:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:116
msgid ""
"the ``sysconfig`` variables ``CC``, ``CXX``, ``CCSHARED``, ``LDSHARED``, and "
"``CFLAGS``,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:118
msgid ""
"the environment variables ``CC``, ``CPP``, ``CXX``, ``LDSHARED`` and "
"``CFLAGS``, ``CPPFLAGS``, ``LDFLAGS``,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:121
msgid ""
"the ``Extension`` attributes ``include_dirs``, ``library_dirs``, "
"``extra_compile_args``, ``extra_link_args``, ``runtime_library_dirs``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:127
msgid ""
"Specifically, if the environment variables ``CC``, ``CPP``, ``CXX``, and "
"``LDSHARED`` are set, they will be used instead of the ``sysconfig`` "
"variables of the same names."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:130
msgid "The compiler options appear in the command line in the following order:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:134
msgid ""
"first, the options provided by the environment variables ``CFLAGS`` and "
"``CPPFLAGS``,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:135
msgid "then, the options provided by the ``sysconfig`` variable ``CCSHARED``,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:136
msgid "then, a ``-I`` option for each element of ``Extension.include_dirs``,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:137
msgid "finally, the options provided by ``Extension.extra_compile_args``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:139
msgid "The linker options appear in the command line in the following order:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:143
msgid ""
"first, the options provided by environment variables and ``sysconfig`` "
"variables,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:144
msgid "then, a ``-L`` option for each element of ``Extension.library_dirs``,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:145
msgid ""
"then, a linker-specific option like ``-Wl,-rpath`` for each element of "
"``Extension.runtime_library_dirs``,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:146
msgid "finally, the options provided by ``Extension.extra_link_args``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:148
msgid ""
"The resulting command line is then processed by the compiler and linker. "
"According to the GCC manual sections on `directory options`_ and "
"`environment variables`_, the C/C++ compiler searches for files named in "
"``#include <file>`` directives in the following order:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:153
msgid "first, in directories given by ``-I`` options (in left-to-right order),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:154
msgid ""
"then, in directories given by the environment variable ``CPATH`` (in left-to-"
"right order),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:155
msgid ""
"then, in directories given by ``-isystem`` options (in left-to-right order),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:156
msgid ""
"then, in directories given by the environment variable ``C_INCLUDE_PATH`` "
"(for C) and ``CPLUS_INCLUDE_PATH`` (for C++),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:157
msgid "then, in standard system directories,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:158
msgid ""
"finally, in directories given by ``-idirafter`` options (in left-to-right "
"order)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:160
msgid "The linker searches for libraries in the following order:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:162
msgid "first, in directories given by ``-L`` options (in left-to-right order),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:163
msgid ""
"then, in directories given by the environment variable ``LIBRARY_PATH`` (in "
"left-to-right order)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:167
msgid "Distributing Extensions compiled with Cython"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:169
msgid ""
"When your :pypi:`Cython` extension modules *are declared using the* :class:"
"`setuptools.Extension` *class*, ``setuptools`` will detect at build time "
"whether Cython is installed or not."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:173
msgid ""
"If Cython is present, then ``setuptools`` will use it to build the ``.pyx`` "
"files. Otherwise, ``setuptools`` will try to find and compile the equivalent "
"``.c`` files (instead of ``.pyx``). These files can be generated using the "
"`cython command line tool`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:178
msgid ""
"You can ensure that Cython is always automatically installed into the build "
"environment by including it as a :ref:`build dependency <build-requires>` in "
"your ``pyproject.toml``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:182
msgid ""
"[build-system]\n"
"requires = [\n"
"    # ...,\n"
"    \"cython\",\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:190
msgid ""
"Alternatively, you can include the ``.c`` code that is pre-compiled by "
"Cython into your source distribution, alongside the original ``.pyx`` files "
"(this might save a few seconds when building from an ``sdist``). To improve "
"version compatibility, you probably also want to include current ``.c`` "
"files in your :wiki:`revision control system`, and rebuild them whenever you "
"check changes in for the ``.pyx`` source files. This will ensure that people "
"tracking your project will be able to build it without installing Cython, "
"and that there will be no variation due to small differences in the generate "
"C files. Please checkout our docs on :ref:`controlling files in the "
"distribution` for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:205
msgid "Extension API Reference"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:1
msgid "Describes a single extension module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:3
msgid ""
"This means that all source files will be compiled into a single binary file "
"``<module path>.<suffix>`` (with ``<module path>`` derived from ``name`` and "
"``<suffix>`` defined by one of the values in ``importlib.machinery."
"EXTENSION_SUFFIXES``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:8
msgid ""
"In the case ``.pyx`` files are passed as ``sources and`` ``Cython`` is "
"**not** installed in the build environment, ``setuptools`` may also try to "
"look for the equivalent ``.cpp`` or ``.c`` files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/ext_modules.rst:0
msgid "Parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:12
msgid ""
"the full name of the extension, including any packages -- ie. *not* a "
"filename or pathname, but Python dotted name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:16
msgid ""
"iterable of source filenames, (except strings, which could be misinterpreted "
"as a single filename), relative to the distribution root (where the setup "
"script lives), in Unix form (slash-separated) for portability.  Source files "
"may be C, C++, SWIG (.i), platform-specific resource files, or whatever else "
"is recognized by the \"build_ext\" command as source for a Python extension."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:24
msgid ""
"list of directories to search for C/C++ header files (in Unix form for "
"portability)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:28
msgid ""
"list of macros to define; each macro is defined using a 2-tuple: the first "
"item corresponding to the name of the macro and the second item either a "
"string with its value or None to define it without a particular value "
"(equivalent of \"#define FOO\" in source or -DFOO on Unix C compiler command "
"line)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:35
msgid "list of macros to undefine explicitly"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:38
msgid "list of directories to search for C/C++ libraries at link time"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:41
msgid "list of library names (not filenames or paths) to link against"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:44
msgid ""
"list of directories to search for C/C++ libraries at run time (for shared "
"extensions, this is when the extension is loaded). Setting this will cause "
"an exception during build on Windows platforms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:50
msgid ""
"list of extra files to link with (eg. object files not implied by 'sources', "
"static library that must be explicitly specified, binary resource files, "
"etc.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:55
msgid ""
"any extra platform- and compiler-specific information to use when compiling "
"the source files in 'sources'.  For platforms and compilers where \"command "
"line\" makes sense, this is typically a list of command-line arguments, but "
"for other platforms it could be anything."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:62
msgid ""
"any extra platform- and compiler-specific information to use when linking "
"object files together to create the extension (or to create a new static "
"Python interpreter).  Similar interpretation as for 'extra_compile_args'."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:68
msgid ""
"list of symbols to be exported from a shared extension.  Not used on all "
"platforms, and not generally necessary for Python extensions, which "
"typically export exactly one symbol: \"init\" + extension_name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:74
msgid ""
"any extra options to pass to SWIG if a source file has the .i extension."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:78
msgid "list of files that the extension depends on"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:81
msgid ""
"extension language (i.e. \"c\", \"c++\", \"objc\"). Will be detected from "
"the source extensions if not provided."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:85
msgid ""
"specifies that a build failure in the extension should not abort the build "
"process, but simply not install the failing extension."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/extension.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.extension.Extension:89
msgid ""
"opt-in flag for the usage of :doc:`Python's limited API <python:c-api/"
"stable>`."
msgstr ""

